# Support RDFLib algebra reassignment and Python solution-row iteration

Preparation draft only. Substantive implementation acceptance is PASS; final hosted qualification, PR feedback, integration assessment and protected merge remain pending. This is not the final squash-notes file.

The RDFLib compatibility consumers previously rejected reassignment forms or evaluated their context and mappings incorrectly. Compile those forms through an explicit first-party Rust algebra route that carries the required context, mapping and initial-binding laws into the existing shared evaluator. Graph, processor, Dataset, ConjunctiveGraph and shadow consumers select that route; ordinary native query entry points retain their established contracts.

The core compiler covers nested EXISTS, OPTIONAL retry, MINUS, grouping and projection, their combinations and no-initial-binding collisions, ordered/bag delivery, empty grouping/projection, mapping-domain hygiene, duplicate publication and callback/error continuation. The consumer route reuses existing binding conversion, frozen snapshots, engine configuration, detached execution and materialization. Named/default-union views select the intended dataset; reached single-graph GRAPH operations raise their typed error. Duplicate Python result labels preserve positional cells and use the final label for named access. Maintained query/interop documentation, translations, stubs and changelog describe the shipped route.

Native QuerySolution iteration lazily yields values in projection order, with Python None for unbound cells. Each iterator independently retains its immutable row owner; repeated iteration and iteration after release of the originating handles remain valid. Native and compatibility rows cover bound, unbound, empty-projection and SELECT-star behavior while preserving actual positional/name access, len and compatibility asdict APIs. The stubs describe the real iterator boundary. Adoption of preserved row work resolves #473 within this combined change; no native asdict API is invented.

Original first-party implementation uses shared parser/evaluator/helper homes. No upstream implementation bodies or coefficient tables, new runtime dependencies, semantic features or separate evaluator were introduced. Locked upstream behavior and unchanged fixture data supply evidence only.

The final projection kernel is authored once in the evaluator's private modifier module and expanded into its two actual callers. It binds inputs once, uses existing Vec/SmallVec bulk initialization homes and preserves caller-local Lift ownership, layout, work admission, errors and unwind cleanup. Ordinary native query and SHACL consumers retain their strict contracts. No unsafe container guard, duplicate kernel, text-rewrite evaluator or fabricated namespace was added.

